A typical argument for subsidizing various sectors (especially agriculture) is that other countries also subsidize "domestic" producers, so we must keep our own competitive through subsidies as well; while that sounds appealing at first glance, economically speaking this does not help us (as
I discussed earlier
). But now a different question interests me: Why should I help some farmer whom I don't even know be competitive against another farmer whom I also don't know, simply because the first one happens to be Czech? I care about the well-being of people (regardless of state); and I care more about those close to me than those less close, more about acquaintances than strangers – that seems natural and self-evident to me. But it is not clear to me why the well-being of Czechs should matter more to me than the well-being of any other human beings; why should I feel kinship toward someone solely based on how some politicians drew boundary lines on a map?

Do you know the difference between a minarchist (a proponent of a small state) and an anarcho-capitalist? Three months. No, seriously... although many anarcho-capitalists distance themselves from minarchists, claiming they are actually statists (and from the perspective of us anarchists, of course they are), I like minarchists. For one thing, minarchism does not even have to be inconsistent (the person may favor maximizing respect for property rights while believing that the maximum realistically achievable lies in a small state), but above all, the state is so large today that I am utterly convinced those who want to shrink it should pull together; however tempting it might be for some to set themselves apart from the other group and showcase their opinion of which they are duly proud, it helps nothing. There will be plenty of time for battles over whether taxes should be low or nonexistent (
not that there isn't a fundamental difference between the two
) once we succeed in ensuring that they are at least not astronomical.

Many libertarian-minded people feel their blood boil whenever they hear about labor unions; and that is completely understandable... nowadays. But it wasn't always that way; unions originally arose in a free-market environment as (entirely legitimate) associations of employees for collective bargaining with employers – instead of individual workers approaching the employer one by one, they banded together and negotiated as a unit to strengthen their bargaining position. Such efforts are completely legitimate, as are strikes, so long as they take place in a market setting where the employer is free to tell the union to get lost and respond to the strike by hiring replacement workers, which is totally acceptable;
however, the problem is introduced by the state
(as always). At a time when the (perhaps primary) mission of labor unions is to pressure employers through legislation – that is, through the use of state violence and coercion – they become, for all practical purposes, a criminal enterprise, even if many rank-and-file members don't realize this and "mean well."

How does the validity of market laws hold up? It depends on how we define them; and since mainstream economics often defines market laws (of price, supply and demand, competition, and so on) under assumptions of perfect elasticity, perfect information, perfect competition, and the like, one could say they never hold. Austrian economics, however, formulates market laws with reality in mind – without those useless, unrealistic assumptions; but people – accustomed to mainstream economics – mostly know nothing about the Austrian school, so they like to talk about "exceptions" to the laws of supply and demand whenever the market operates substantially differently from idealized macroeconomic models. Yet that doesn't have to be a bad thing! Even an industry where virtually a single firm trades, eliminating all competition, does not have to be "non-market" or bad for consumers (
see Standard Oil
), unless it is caused by state intervention (which is the only thing that genuinely distorts markets).

