Politicians love to stoke "class conflicts": poor versus rich, consumers versus entrepreneurs, employees versus employers, locals versus foreigners, and all sorts of other "us" versus "them" groups. For politicians, such conflicts are beneficial because they feed on them—they can comment on them, offer to "solve" them, pick sides, and reap votes from them. People then forget how extensively and frequently these groups overlap and how closely intertwined they are (
for example, the worse the position of employers, the fewer of them there will be; they will become employees in the labor market, worsening the position of other employees due to increased supply—the law of supply and demand applies here as well
); above all, however, people forget that the true common enemy of all decent people is not other decent people, but the state, which turns us against one another through unjust laws and then profits from it, as confused citizens clamor for "solutions" in the form of further bad laws.

In debates about a free society, it is commonplace for statists to demand that free-market advocates justify why a free-market society would work better than a state-run one; although I typically accept that debate and strive to provide such justifications, it is actually a deeply absurd state of affairs. Why should someone who wishes to leave everyone with what they have earned have to justify their position to someone proposing aggression to seize those funds? Leaving everyone what is rightfully theirs requires no grand justification; on the contrary, using violence to extract resources from others is something that, in my view, cannot be justified at all—but if someone is to attempt it, they should unquestionably bring exceptionally compelling arguments! Merely because the status quo is currently inverted (historically due to various "divine right of kings" rationales), debates unfold backwards;
it is non-aggression that must be defended
.

Interest emerged quite naturally long before economists succeeded in explaining its true nature, as a result of which it was often condemned on nonsensical grounds; and unfortunately, even today many view it as immoral or undesirable. Why does it arise? First, a loan always carries risk (even with the debtor's best intentions, they may fail to repay), and second, we value a "good now" more than a "good later." I deliberately refer to a good in general, not merely money, because interest is not a purely monetary phenomenon, but a universal one: the creditor gives up something, and interest is the fee compensating them for being without it (and for the risk of losing it). In the broader economy, monetary interest is sometimes labeled "unpayable" without the continuous injection of new money; this, however, is a major fallacy, since a single crown can be used to service the same debt multiple times (as the creditor spends it and the debtor earns it again);
there is thus nothing immoral or undesirable about interest
.

Politicians instill in us the notion that we ought to take an interest in politics and "be active"; naturally, they want us to pay attention to them. Knowing who holds which cabinet post, what they look like, and who they are is generally perceived favorably (sometimes people commend me for knowing the structure of the national budget by heart and keeping tabs on politicians—though mostly because of how frequently I critique them); conversely, those who take no interest in politics often feel compelled to apologize for it. But I don't believe they have anything to be ashamed of; nor am I proud of wading through that cesspool so often—it is no virtue. In general, "being active in public life" is not inherently good; one can be "publicly active" in both positive and negative ways. In fact, I appreciate the person who skips elections, minds their own business, and doesn't even know who the president, prime minister, or finance minister is;
if literally everyone were like that, we would have peace
.

A huge number of people hate capitalism, or at least dislike it intensely. Yet it is not entirely clear to me what they actually object to... that each person decides what to do, what not to do, what to buy and not buy, what services to offer and not offer, what contracts to enter, whom to employ or not employ and under what terms? Do they object to people having the freedom to live their lives as they see fit, longing instead to violently coerce them into submitting to the will of the powerful (usually the state)? Do they want to micromanage others' lives purely for the fun of it? Would it please them for us to live in the squalor and destitution caused by the impossibility of economic calculation under central planning? No. I don't believe those people are evil (at least for the most part). They don't want us to suffer. Unfortunately, however, they associate that suffering with capitalism—usually without any deeper rationale or logical analysis. The word itself carries a massive negative connotation;
let's redeem it
!

Although practically everyone admits that war is horrific, many people see a "silver lining" in that wars drive technological and economic development, and post-war reconstruction gives people jobs; why are these so-called "benefits" illusory rather than real? Well, if you walk up to someone and smash their car, thereby forcing them to work overtime to save up for a new one, you may have "stimulated the economy," but it's hard to call that a positive outcome; during a war, the exact same thing happens, just on a massive scale. Faster technological advancement does indeed occur, but because there is no such thing as a free lunch, it comes at the expense of something else. What? The state forcibly extracts resources from people to fund military research; those people can no longer purchase what they would otherwise prefer. For had they truly prioritized technological innovation over other goods, they certainly wouldn't have needed a war to invest in science voluntarily;
if they do not do so, it simply isn't worth it to them
.

"People are not yet mature enough not to need someone to rule over them," many say when criticizing anarchy; and many a worldly cynic nods knowingly, calling to mind all those foolish people (
which isn't actually as dire in reality, as I discussed in a lecture
). But how then can those very same people, who are supposedly not mature enough to take responsibility for their own lives, be capable of responsibly electing their rulers? After all, the difficulty of the task "take care of yourself, pay the bills, make ends meet, and plan at least a little for the future" is orders of magnitude lower than the difficulty of the task "gauge the consequences of your democratic vote"—for the latter involves not only discerning whether the candidate is lying, but primarily assessing the economic and societal repercussions of their platform, all of which are extraordinarily complex and affect not just the voter himself, but the entire society. Do you see the contradiction?

Very often we encounter the argument that although redistribution slows down the overall enrichment of society generated by capitalism, we need to "find a balance" between economic growth and social justice, because we have a duty to take care of the poor even at the expense of our own prosperity. But do the champions of such theories realize all the implications? Consider, in particular, a comparison between the quality of education, healthcare, housing, or food available to the poorest members of society today versus a hundred years ago—a staggering difference, isn't it? And what happens if we slow down this development through redistribution? It may well help someone in the short term (and even that is
debatable
), but at the expense of all those who, at any later point in time, will have to suffer or die simply because the technology that would have solved their problems was not yet available or widespread (due to the slowed-down progress). Every choice carries opportunity costs that cannot be ignored.

I don't like Alza;
they acted like total dicks to me
. What do I do about it? Well, I simply don't shop there; problem solved. Not so the ČOI [Czech Trade Inspection Authority] – Alza didn't do anything to them either, yet they keep going there to harass them; why? Because they are open during public holidays; how dare they? The retailer and its employees voluntarily want to sell goods, people want to buy them—a mutually advantageous deal; and a politician says they're simply out of luck, nothing doing! The Czech Trade Inspection then rushes in like the state's attack pooch, "just doing its job," and sues Alza for a million crowns; what's more, this December incident isn't the first, the exact same thing happened in October. If this e-shop intends to keep doing this and programmatically ignore that law, my antipathy towards them will probably vanish and I'll start buying from them just to support them; and if they maintain a defiant, nonconformist stance on this, I'll probably head there for my very next purchase.
